  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\n(Saturday Night - Labor Day)\n\nDeterministic model guidance is now showing a quicker passage of the \nbackdoor front, moving through much of the area Saturday night \nbefore clearing central Missouri sometime Sunday morning. The \ndewpoint (relative humidity) decrease however lags behind the front \nabout 9-12 hours so areas along/west of the Mississippi River will \nstay humid into Sunday afternoon. Another day of 100-105F peak heat \nindex values are possible for parts of central, east central, and \nsoutheast Missouri. Due to some lingering uncertainty on the timing \nof the front, we have elected to hold off on extending any heat \nheadlines at this time. However, a conversion to an advisory at \nleast for parts of central/southeast Missouri seem more likely than \nnot, but this will be further evaluated over the next 12-24 hours. \n\nLabor Day continues to look like the \"pick\" day of the holiday \nweekend, with cooler temperatures moving in, but more importantly, \nmuch lower dewpoints (humidity levels). The signal for these lower \nhumidity values have increased substantially compared to yesterday \nfrom the LREF. Probabilities for dewpoints below 65F are 70+% now \nareawide, with values in the mid to upper 50s likely (60-80%) \nroughly along/east of the Mississippi River. While high temperatures \nare still expected to be quite warm for early September (upper 80s \nto low 90s), the much lower humidity levels means a definitive \n(albeit fairly short) break from the ongoing heat.\n\n\n(Tuesday - Next Friday)\n\nAfter a brief reprieve from the heat, the record-breaking mid/upper \nlevel ridge shifts back east into the mid-Mississippi Valley by \nTuesday. Temperatures will increase back into the mid to upper 90s, \nthough the good news is humidity levels will be slower to increase. \nTherefore, at least a majority of the area should see heat index \nvalues stay below the century mark. \n\nWednesday continues to have the best more potential for possible \nrecord-breaking heat ahead of an approaching cold front. Speaking of \nwhich, there has been a notable shift in both the GEFS/EPS toward a \nstronger midlevel trough moving across the Great Lakes. This has led \nto increased confidence that the midweek cold front will move \nthrough the area, the question is exactly when? Guidance has about a \n12 hour spread, with late Tuesday night/Wednesday morning on the \nearlier end of the spectrum with Wednesday afternoon/night on the \nlater end. This spread with respect to the frontal timing is why \nthere is low confidence with specific high temperatures for \nWednesday. However, IF the front is slow enough to not impact \ntemperatures, I still think that Wednesday may be the hottest day of \nthe month with widespread upper 90s to low 100s possible. In terms \nof precipitation chances, the frontal passage is probably are best \nchance (20-30%) for seeing showers and thunderstorms. \n\nCooler temperatures are likely behind the midweek cold front, but \nhow much cooler is uncertain. Given recent trends in ensemble \nguidance, it appears more likely that we should see highs in the 80s \nfor the first time since late August for Thursday and next Friday. \nThere is some bad news though for anyone hoping for Fall. The large-\nscale mid/upper air pattern across the CONUS characterized by a \nlarge omega block does not look to change. Furthermore, ensemble \nguidance suggests the ridge may move back into the lower to mid \nMississippi Valley. If this indeed were to happen, another bout of \nwell-above normal temperatures would likely occur. However, before \nthat may occur, our area may be in a more favorable spot (on the \nnortheast periphery of the ridge) late next week for a possible \n\"ridge-runner\" or two. Given how dry it has been the past couple of \nweeks, any rain would be a welcome sight for most.",
